// @run
// @log a!
// @log 7
// The SILENT WRONG ANSWER half of #1265, and the reason the fix is not merely a
// crash fix: here the mis-resolution produced a running program with the wrong output.
//
// A nested CONCRETE `id` shadows a top-level `id<T>`. The call `id("a")` inside `host`
// must reach the nested one and print `a!`. The monomorphizer's flat `fnIndexOf` instead
// credited the call to the top-level generic, instantiated `id<T>` at `string`, and — since
// `monoInstantiate` rewrites the callee `Ident` IN PLACE to the instance name — REWIRED the
// call site away from the shadowing declaration. The program printed `a`: the generic's
// identity body, not the nested `+ "!"` one. rc 0, valid wasm, wrong answer.
//
// The i32 arrangement of this same shape (a nested `id` returning `n + 100`) printed the
// CORRECT `101` on the pre-fix compiler, because instance 0 of `id<T>` keeps the bare name
// `id` and the emitter's scope walk still found the nested slot first. Which of the two a
// program got was luck; both are correct now.
function id<T>(x: T): T { return x }

function host(): string {
  function id(s: string): string { return s + "!" }
  return id("a")
}

print(host())
print(id(7))
